  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/883a60e9ac0d526d76243a44cff129f04ca148c1c2f0fb6a4b34db630b2193cd.png)\n\nThe differential equation that generates the slope field shown above is given by ${y}^{\u2032}=(x-a)(x-b).$ Find the value of $a+b.$",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044310/assets/012fe788fa1ac778b361fe62c3f70344546c6c5008fa6dcce536cc721d4ba5e9.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=-2$ and $x=1,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nSo, our differential equation must be\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=(x+2)(x-1).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=-2,b=1$ and $a+b=-1.$",
